= Introduction <sec:introduction>

This document is a sample project shipped with vswrite. It is meant to be
opened, scrolled through, edited, broken, and rebuilt. Treat it as a
playground that happens to also be a small thesis on its own subject:
how to write academic texts in an era when AI assistance is part of the
workflow whether we acknowledge it or not.

== Who this is for

There are three groups of readers we had in mind:

- *Researchers and graduate students* who want to draft, structure, and
  cite their work without context-switching between five tools.
- *Authors of longer non-fiction* who appreciate that scientific tooling
  (citations, cross-references, footnotes) is also useful outside of
  academia.
- *Anyone curious* about what it looks like when a writing app is built
  with AI-assisted workflows in mind from the start, rather than bolted
  on after the fact.

== What you will see

Every feature of vswrite — comments, cross-references, footnotes,
citations, math, code blocks, figures — appears at least once in this
document. The intent is that by the time you reach the conclusion, you
have seen the tool's full vocabulary in context. We try not to repeat
the user manual; for setup details, configuration, and edge cases see
the handbook at #link("https://vswrite.netlify.app/en/docs")[vswrite.netlify.app/en/docs].

The substantive thread running through the chapters is the one announced
above: how to integrate AI into academic writing without losing the
qualities that make academic writing worth reading. We draw on five
recent open-access sources @bender2021parrots @chen2021codex @weidinger2021risks @ji2022hallucination @liu2023chatgpt
to ground the discussion in something more durable than vendor blog
posts. All five PDFs ship with the sample under `sources/` so you can
hover any citation in the editor and open the original paper inline.

== A note on tone

vswrite was built around one specific belief: AI is at its best when it
takes the tedious parts off the writer's plate — formatting, citation
hunting, table conversion, draft polishing — so the writer can spend
more time on the part nobody else can do for them, which is having
something worth saying. That belief shows up in the rest of this
document. You will not find AI maximalism or AI dismissiveness here;
both are easy and both are wrong.
